-- title:
--   Translation preserves convexity
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a real normed vector space, let $S \subseteq F$ be a convex set, and let $x \in F$. Write $S[x]$ for the translate of $S$ by $x$,
--   $$S[x] = x + S = \{\, x + s \;:\; s \in S \,\},$$
--   the operation written `shift S x` in the formalization. Then
--   $$S \text{ convex} \;\Longrightarrow\; S[x] \text{ convex}.$$
--
--   The result is the translation-invariance half of the elementary calculus of shifted sets. It is what allows a cone defined with apex at the origin to be moved to an arbitrary apex without losing convexity, so that every convexity statement proved for cones at the origin transfers verbatim to shifted cones.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is proved at the generality of a normed additive commutative group carrying a real vector space structure, not for cones in Euclidean space specifically; no hypothesis is placed on $x$ or on the dimension.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Translate.lean#L40-L49

